{"title":"Ishika's Compass Adventure","gradeSubjectTag":"Class 5 · Science · Magnets and their poles","readMinutes":2,"scenes":[{"label":"hook","text":"Ishika was helping her grandmother set up a new sewing basket for Diwali when she found an old compass in the cupboard. 'Grandmother, this needle keeps pointing the same way no matter which way I turn it,' she said, curious."},{"label":"problem","text":"Ishika wanted to understand why the needle always pointed north, and she wanted to arrange some iron pins on the sewing table using magnets to make patterns. But the magnets weren't working the way she expected—sometimes they stuck to the pins strongly, sometimes they didn't, and sometimes they pushed each other away."},{"label":"mathMoment","text":"Grandmother showed her that every magnet has two poles: uthara dhruva (north pole) and dakshin dhruva (south pole). 'See,' said Grandmother, 'when you bring two magnets close with the same poles facing each other—north to north or south to south—they push apart. But when you bring the opposite poles together—north to south—they pull towards each other and stick tightly.' Ishika tried this, and the magnets jumped together with a click when she matched the right poles."},{"label":"wrapUp","text":"Now Ishika understood! She held the magnet with its uthara dhruva pointing at the compass needle, and the needle spun away. Then she flipped it so the dakshin dhruva pointed at the needle, and it jumped to meet the magnet. Using this knowledge, she made beautiful patterns with the iron pins, arranging them where the magnet's poles were strongest. 'The poles are the superstars of magnets!' she told Grandmother happily."}],"visual":null,"equations":[],"tryTogether":{"question":"You have two bar magnets. The first magnet's uthara dhruva is facing you. If you want the second magnet to stick to it, which pole—uthara dhruva or dakshin dhruva—should you point towards the first magnet?","answer":"The dakshin dhruva (south pole) of the second magnet should point towards the first magnet, because opposite poles attract."},"parentPrompt":"Bring two fridge magnets together and ask Ishika which poles are attracting and which are repelling by feeling the push and pull.","concept_key":"c5-science-magnet-poles","scene_prompt":"A girl discovers that invisible forces at magnet ends push and pull in opposite ways."}
